icipants  reported few adverse events and they  were generally mild  most common ones being  gastrointestinal related  and the last thing is  looking at the products in our studies  it appears that the health outcomes and  the effects might be contingent upon the  amount of cbd per serving and  paradoxically  more cbd per serving is not necessarily  better  and  whereas when we looked at for example  the spectrum broad isolate  and full we didn't necessarily find  any significant difference when we  grouped products that way  now on the cbd  serving side there's some interesting  animal studies  and  a handful of human studies that have  also  shown that more cbd is not necessarily  better  that there is a moderate dose of cbd  that has better  effects than either a high dose  or a low dose  and so  that this has been demonstrated again in  both mostly animal studies but there's  also a handful of human studies that  have played with different doses and  found that the optimal effectiveness  actually came from an intermediate or  not intermediate dose from an absolute  term but came from a  dose that was in the middle of several  doses that they were exploring  so with that we will  open it up to questions  great  thank you dr chen for your informative  presentation and we will now start the q  a portion of the webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  so let's get started  our first question is how and when do  you think regulation on cbd will change  so  there have been  several attempts in congress to actually  pass a law  that would  make cbd a dietary supplement and would  subject it to the same regulations as  all  dietary supplements and so  these proposed bills in the past have  gotten bipartisan support  in fact  the  push for hemp legalization was actually  driven  predominantly  by  republican  uh politicians  who  had a lot of agricultural constituents  who wanted a new cash crop  that wasn't tobacco that wasn't corn and  hemp is much more valuable as a cash  crop if you can actually use the  cannabinoids from it if you just grow it  for fiber or seed it's it's worth  a tiny fraction than if you can grow it  for cannabinoids so i think there are  there is there is bipartisan support as  well  for  regulation that will clarify and make  cbd  a supplement  because  the retailers want it consumers want it  and even the farmers want it  as well so i wouldn't be surprised if  it's going to take it's going to be a  congressional act that's going to  add clarity and full legality to the use  of cbd as a supplement i don't think  it's going to be the fda suddenly  changing  its stance  and as for when that'll happen  you know the best you know there's a  saying that's like never predict the  future if you don't want to be wrong  so uh as for when it'll happen who knows  man i think  uh they were trying to get another bill  introduced this year but it sounds like  there's just some other more pressing  concerns right now in congress so it  might not might still be years before we  get any clear  clarity on the eye on the issue  all right thank you for that  another question we have is what are the  cbd dosages that were associated with  greater chances for achieving clinically  meaningful reductions in symptoms  right so  and  i i need to go back and take a look  at the data set but if you see let's see  if i can jump to it  so if you look at  it was actually the the doses below 15  milligrams that  were often outperforming the higher  doses and again  dose is not the end-all be-all  because these products differ in other  attributes as well now if all the  products were the same they were all  full spectrum gummies that just differed  in dose then you could say oh  interesting like here's what the the  dose showed but in this case the  products differed in a lot of areas but  as far as we can tell when we just  looked at dose setting aside the other  attributes  we found that you know products with  less than 15 milligrams per serving  were  often outperforming the products at  higher ones and we had products in the  study that had servings of  you know over 25 milligrams over 50 all  the way approaching  100 but again this is just on average  and it wasn't all the time that the  lower doses are performed the higher  doses  now  what's interesting is if you look at the  clinical trials that have been done on  cbd showing benefits for epilepsy  schizophrenia addiction  and social anxiety for example  they were testing doses  hundreds of milligrams a day all the way  up to a thousand or in excess of a  thousand milligrams a day  the published literature you know  blinded randomized controlled clinical  trials on cbd  has so so so rarely studied less than  you know 200  milligrams and has pretty much not  studied  2035 you know all these other values  so there is a big  gap in the literature  and but again looking at some of the  animal studies and some of the human  studies there's this notion that hey  more cbd might not necessarily be  better  and it's not just cbd this is not an  uncommon phenomenon pharmacology  where in the in the dosage window there  sometimes is a  moderate dose that's actually most  optimal  thank you for that  and our next question is what evidence  is there for a so-called entourage  effect  right so  it primarily comes from  animal studies  where  they would give the the mice  say pure cbd  versus a  temper cannabis extract that had a same  amount of cbd so the dose was the same  but that extract had all the other stuff  in the plant other terpenes other  cannabinoids of their vital compounds  and they found pretty distinct  differences in their effects and  outcomes so a few studies that come to  mind they looked at inflammation in rats  and they found that even when matched  for the same dose of cbd the full  spectrum the plant extract significantly  outperformed  a same dose of cbd when it was just cbd  by itself had much better  anti-inflammatory effect  also when looking at  studies in cancer cell lines again not  even animal but like a petri dish  studies a huge caveat but they would  take for instance pure thc  and expose the cells in the tumor cells  in the petri dish to the pure thc  and then they would expose  the tumor cells to a cannabis extract  that had the same amount of thc but  again had a bunch of other stuff in it  and the cannabis extract had  significantly better anti-cancer  activity the pure thc was able to kill a  certain percentage of the cancer cells  but the cannabis extract was able to  kill significantly more  and so  the real question  for this the so-called entourage effect  first off you know is it present in  humans and also  you know there's so many different  types of cannabis and they're grown so  differently  it's  even if this turns out to be true it's  not going to be true  all the time if that makes any sense  because every  extract of cannabis is a completely  different  assortment of  dozens if not hundreds of physically  active  compounds  and so there's been some work by  daddy marie out of israel where he took  a cannabis extract  and then sequentially  purified it more and more and more and  each time he did a round of purification  he would expose in this case again with  cancer cells he'd expose cancer cells to  a extract that was getting increasingly  purified  until he found that the effects started  to change then he would know oh well  these were the  you know five compounds that were  purified out and the effects changed  maybe one of those five was a key  constituent so a big question for the  entourage effect is do you need hundreds  do you need dozens do you need is it  like three key compounds  in the cannabis plant that will  effectively create the effect that the  whole extract  will create and if you look at dede's  work at least when it comes to cancer  cell lines it looks like the answer is  somewhere between like  two and  eight  two to eight  ingredients  are critical and can effectively  approximate the entire plant  extract but again human studies are  still pretty lacking right now aside  from this  anecdotal observation that pure thc has  been fda approved  for  you know almost 40 years yet no one  really uses it everyone gravitates  towards medical cannabis you know  visit both physicians and patients so  thank you for that response and then we  can try to wrap up with this last  question i don't know if there'll be a  set answer but somebody was curious if  there was a reason the steady duration  wasn't a little bit longer  sure yeah so part of the reason  for the study duration was just the the  speed with which we wanted to launch and  complete the study and get data out  there  you know when we  launched the study our company was only  a few months old so we launched the  study  last summer and it was done before  christmas  so you know pretty unprecedented this  this is a study that should have taken  you know whatever five years we got it  done in six months  so part of that was we kept the study  duration short so that we could actually  finish the study quicker and get the  results back to  the brands in the study and get the  results back to society faster  furthermore we had a suspicion that  based on some prior preliminary studies  we've done  that the effects would happen in the  first couple weeks and then we kind of  plateau off so we could have done a  longer study i don't think there would  have been that much of a difference  between say day 30 and day 60 like i  don't think i don't think their pain  would have gotten that much better or  worse beyond how it already changed in  the first 30 days  but certainly there is a role for longer  term  studies and and what the fda is asking  for is longer term studies where they  specifically get to track  liver function  um  but again a long-term study on a lot of  people  tracking liver function it's super super  expensive right now no one's really  stepped up to  pay for that especially if that would be  a study done on non-prescription  products that you can't really patent  and monopolize the same way that you can  with a pharma product so that's why  there's a lot more safety data on  epidiolex right the fda approved cbd  product  than  you know these commercial products  because  the the there really isn't these  companies don't have the budgets  to pay for you know multi-million dollar  long-term safety studies unfortunately  it's not it's not their fault it's just  that type of research is really  expensive  that makes sense thank you for your  insight  um thank you overall dr chen for your  informative presentation  for your time today and your important  research and before we'd go i'd like to  thank the audience for joining us today  and for their interesting questions  questions we do not have time for today  and those submitting during the  on-demand period will be addressed by  the speaker via the contact information  you provided at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand for  two years until march 23 2024 and lab  routes will alert you via email when  it's available for replay we encourage  you to share that email with any  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event  thank you again until next time goodbye  thanks for having me bye  you

